Overview:
  The Gamification Rules Engine evaluates declarative rule definitions
  against user state and events to determine point awards, badge unlocks,
  and other gamification actions. Rules are versioned, auditable, and
  support time-window conditions, streak tracking, and dependency chains.
  The engine is async and non-blocking: it consumes events and emits
  outcomes without delaying the main user flow.

Rules:

  [GR1] All gamification rules MUST be declarative (JSON/YAML), not hardcoded.

  [GR2] Rules MUST have unique IDs and semantic versioning (rule_id, version).

  [GR3] Rule evaluation MUST be async and non-blocking with bounded timeout (max 5s).

  [GR4] Rules MUST be idempotent: same input + same rule version = same output.

  [GR5] Every rule evaluation MUST be logged: rule_id, user_id, input, output, timestamp.

  [GR6] Rules MAY depend on other rules (prerequisites) but MUST NOT create cycles.

  [GR7] Time-window rules MUST use server time, not client time.

  [GR8] Rules MUST support graceful degradation: if evaluation fails, emit default outcome.

  [GR9] Rule authors MUST validate rules before activation (syntax, logic, performance).

  [GR10] Deprecated rules MUST be archived, not deleted, for auditability.

  [GR11] Rule changes MUST be gradual: shadow mode, then canary, then full rollout.

  [GR12] Rules MUST NOT access external systems directly; use integration modules.

  [GR13] Streak rules MUST handle timezone-aware day boundaries.

  [GR14] Daily limit rules MUST be evaluated before point awards, not after.

  [GR15] Rule conflicts MUST be resolved by explicit priority or last-write-wins with audit.

Architecture:

  RulesEngine:
    owns:
      - Rule registry and versioning
      - Condition evaluation
      - Action execution
      - Rule dependency resolution
      - Audit logging
      - Campaign management

    api:
      - evaluate(event, userState) -> {actions: [{type, params}]}
      - activate(ruleId, version) -> void
      - deactivate(ruleId) -> void
      - getStatus(ruleId) -> {active, version, stats}

    deps:
      - event-processor (input events)
      - integration-module (external data)
      - cache (user state, rate limits)

RuleStructure:
  rule:
    id: string (unique)
    version: string (semver)
    name: string
    description: string
    status: draft | active | shadow | deprecated
    priority: number (higher wins on conflict)
    conditions:
      - type: action_count | user_attribute | time_window | streak | external
        operator: eq | neq | gt | lt | gte | lte | in | not_in | exists
        field: string
        value: any
        window: daily | weekly | monthly | all_time | custom
        timezone: string (IANA)
    actions:
      - type: award_points | unlock_badge | notify | unlock_content
        params: object
        limit: { per: user | action | day, max: number }
    dependencies:
      - rule_id: string
        version: string (optional, defaults to latest active)
    metadata:
      campaign_id: string
      author: string
      created_at: ISO8601
      valid_from: ISO8601
      valid_until: ISO8601

EvaluationWorkflow:
  1. Receive event (action_performed, user_id, context)
  2. Load user state from cache/DB (attributes, recent actions, badges)
  3. Filter active rules by event type match
  4. Evaluate conditions in priority order
  5. Resolve dependencies (evaluate prerequisite rules first)
  6. Execute actions for matched rules (async)
  7. Log evaluation result with rule_id, version, outcome
  8. Emit outcome events for downstream processing
  9. Update rule statistics (evaluations, matches, errors)

TimeWindowRules:
  daily:
    boundary: midnight-to-midnight in user timezone
    storage: Redis sorted set with date key
    aggregation: count, sum, max, min
  weekly:
    boundary: Monday-to-Sunday in user timezone
    storage: Redis sorted set with week key
  monthly:
    boundary: 1st-to-last day in user timezone
    storage: Redis sorted set with month key
  custom:
    boundary: defined in rule config (start_hour, start_day)
    storage: Redis sorted set with custom key

StreakRules:
  definition: consecutive days with qualifying action
  calculation:
    - fetch last N action dates for user
    - check continuity with today/yesterday
    - handle timezone-aware day boundaries
  storage:
    - current_streak: number
    - longest_streak: number
    - last_action_date: ISO8601 date
    - streak_frozen: boolean (pause protection)
  maintenance:
    - recalculate on each qualifying action
    - reset if gap > 1 day (unless frozen)
    - snapshot at season end

CampaignManagement:
  campaign:
    id: string
    name: string
    rules: [rule_id]
    status: draft | scheduled | active | paused | ended
    start_date: ISO8601
    end_date: ISO8601
    targeting:
      user_segments: [segment_id]
      countries: [ISO3166]
      platforms: [ios, android, web]
    allocation:
      variant_a: 50%
      variant_b: 50%
    measurement:
      metrics: [participation_rate, completion_rate, retention]
      success_criteria: object

RuleConflicts:
  detection:
    - same event type matches multiple active rules
    - same action triggered by multiple rules
  resolution:
    - priority field (higher wins)
    - explicit conflict resolution config
    - last-write-wins with full audit trail
  logging:
    - conflict_id
    - competing rules
    - resolution strategy
    - timestamp

Performance:
  rule_evaluation_p99: < 50ms
  rules_per_event: < 10 (default), max 50
  rule_cache_ttl: 5m (active rules), 1h (deprecated rules)
  batch_evaluation: up to 1000 events per batch
  parallel_evaluation: per-user parallelism enabled

Best Practices:

  - Use rule templates for common patterns (daily_login, referral, purchase)
  - Implement rule dry-run mode for testing before activation
  - Cache compiled rules in Redis for hot-path performance
  - Use feature flags for rule activation per user segment
  - Monitor rule evaluation latency and error rates
  - Implement circuit breaker for rules requiring external data
  - Support rule inheritance and composition (base rules + overrides)
  - Log rule evaluation with full context for debugging
  - Implement gradual rollout: 1% -> 10% -> 50% -> 100%
  - Support A/B testing with rule variants and allocation groups

Anti-Patterns:

  - Hardcoded rules in application code
  - Synchronous rule evaluation in user-facing paths
  - Rules with side effects on external systems
  - Circular rule dependencies
  - Rules without timeout or circuit breaker
  - Deleted rules (must archive)
  - Rule evaluation without audit logging
  - Global rules without user segmentation

